+++ b/docs/architecture/j2k-reset.md
@@ -26,9 +26,9 @@ The J2K backup pipeline is an intentional narrow adapter seam: `Backup` owns one
 
 Extension discovery uses one Hayai-owned `ContentKind` policy for browse, search, reader routing, and migration. Extension management deliberately does not partition the catalog by content kind. `ManagedExtensionProjector` unions J2K APK state and LNReader JavaScript plugin state into one Extensions list with namespaced `apk:` and `js:` identities, format and content badges, and shared Update, Installed, and Available sections. Installation, update, and health are orthogonal, so an installed plugin can remain visible while its runtime is unhealthy or its repository is offline. Language and adult-source filters apply only to remote available entries; installed, untrusted, and load-failed packages remain manageable. J2K continues to own APK loading, signature trust, package installation, source registration, and update semantics. `NovelPluginManager` continues to own JavaScript repository trust, checksums, signing keys, QuickJS warm-up, atomic storage, and semantic versions.
 
-APK repository URLs remain in J2K's single `extensionRepos` preference. Hayai stores only novel membership and novel-only ownership tags. Manga-only, novel-only, and shared ownership transitions are idempotent: removing one view never deletes a URL or its metadata while the other view still owns it. Repository redirects migrate both J2K URLs and Hayai tags together. Browse and Browse settings expose one repository destination with explicit APK-index and LNReader JavaScript sections. The distinct repository formats remain visible because their trust models differ, but neither section contains a second extension catalog.
+APK repository URLs remain in J2K's single `extensionRepos` preference. Hayai stores novel membership tags only for backup, restore, and redirect compatibility. The unified APK repository section lists every global APK index, regardless of content kind; adding, removing, or replacing a URL updates the global set and Hayai tags together. Browse and Browse settings expose one repository destination with explicit APK-index and LNReader JavaScript sections. The distinct repository formats remain visible because their trust models differ, but neither section contains a second extension catalog.
 
-LNReader-compatible JavaScript sources use a separate Hayai-owned trust boundary. `AppModule` registers one manager. `SourceManager` merges the manager's source flow with J2K's authoritative APK source flow and rejects source-ID collisions. Repository documents and plugin downloads are bounded. `NovelPluginRepositoryDocument` normalizes current root arrays and legacy `plugins` or `sources` envelopes, removes invalid optional catalogue websites, and percent-encodes raw path brackets before URI validation. Remote repositories require HTTPS, optional publisher checksums are enforced, and installed code and metadata are published atomically. Each source owns a serialized QuickJS runtime with evaluation, response-size, and cache limits. Installation initializes the runtime before code becomes active. Android application context now enters explicitly through `NovelPluginManager`, `NovelPluginSource`, `NovelPluginRuntime`, and `NovelPluginLibrary`; the runtime never resolves an unregistered raw `Context` from global DI. Runtime construction failures remain attached to installed plugin IDs in the unified catalog instead of disappearing. The bridge exposes only the compatibility APIs needed by LNReader plugins, including network, Cheerio and Jsoup, storage, timers, crypto, URL, and protobuf helpers. It does not expose Android APIs or app lifecycle APIs.
+LNReader-compatible JavaScript sources use a separate Hayai-owned trust boundary. `AppModule` registers one manager. `SourceManager` merges the manager's source flow with J2K's authoritative APK source flow and rejects source-ID collisions. Repository documents and plugin downloads are bounded. `NovelPluginRepositoryDocument` normalizes current root arrays and legacy `plugins` or `sources` envelopes, removes invalid optional catalogue websites, and percent-encodes raw path brackets before URI validation. Remote repositories require HTTPS, optional publisher checksums are enforced, and installed code and metadata are published atomically. Each source owns a serialized QuickJS runtime with evaluation, response-size, and cache limits. Installation initializes the runtime before code becomes active. Android application context now enters explicitly through `NovelPluginManager`, `NovelPluginSource`, `NovelPluginRuntime`, and `NovelPluginLibrary`; the runtime never resolves an unregistered raw `Context` from global DI. Source construction and asynchronous warm-up failures remain attached to the current installed plugin ID in the unified catalog instead of disappearing. The bridge exposes only the compatibility APIs needed by LNReader plugins, including network, Cheerio and Jsoup, storage, timers, crypto, URL, and protobuf helpers. It does not expose Android APIs or app lifecycle APIs.
 
